*
* START-DESCRIPTION:
*
*    rc = !elevate(action, arg)
*
*    action  START   obtain privilege for this session.  UAC PROMPTS HERE.
*            RUN     run a script elevated; arg is its pathname, and the
*                    return value is that script's own exit code
*            STOP    give the privilege up
*
*    returns 0 done
*            1 the operation failed
*            5 not elevated: refused at the prompt, or no way to ask
*            9 no privilege has been obtained for this session
*
*    THERE IS NO ELEVATE VERB AND THERE MUST NOT BE.  Owner's rule, 16 Aug
*    2026: "there should never be an elevation from within a normal session".
*    Privilege comes from entering SDSYS and from nowhere else.  This is a
*    subroutine, not a command, and nothing puts it in a VOC.
*
*    29 Aug 26 Windows port - THERE ARE NOW TWO CALLERS, NOT ONE.  This said
*    "the only caller of START and STOP is CPROC's LOGTO handling"; LOGIN is
*    the second, because the owner's model of 29 Aug 2026 elevates an
*    administrator AT LOGIN and lands them in SDSYS.  PRE_RELEASE_FIXES 56.
*
*    THE RULE ABOVE IS UNTOUCHED BY THAT.  "Within a normal session" is what
*    it forbids, and LOGIN runs before there is a session to be within: it is
*    deciding which account the session begins in.  Privilege still comes from
*    entering SDSYS and from nowhere else - only the door has moved.  If a
*    THIRD caller is ever proposed, that is the sentence to weigh it against,
*    and the answer is very probably no.
*
*    WHY A HELPER PROCESS RATHER THAN AN ELEVATED SD.  A Windows process's
*    token is fixed when the process is created and nothing can elevate a
*    running one, so SD stays unelevated for its whole life and a separate
*    elevated process does the privileged work.  That is the smaller exposure
*    of the two: in an elevated terminal everything typed runs privileged,
*    whereas here only what SD sends does.
*
*    START IS A NO-OP WHEN THE SESSION IS ALREADY ELEVATED, which is how the
*    installer's own account step, a bootstrap and "sd -internal" keep working
*    untouched - they have privilege already, there is nothing to ask for, and
*    no prompt appears.  !ps_script then runs its script itself, as always.
*
*    IT CANNOT WORK OVER ssh, AND THAT IS THE POINT.  UAC draws its consent
*    dialog on the interactive desktop and an ssh session has none, so START
*    fails there and the LOGTO that called it is refused.  Administrator work
*    stays at the console or on a remote-control tool - PROJECT_STATUS.md
*    5.6.2 - enforced by Windows rather than by a test here that could drift.
*
*    THE HELPER IS TAKEN FROM Program Files AND NOT FROM THE DATA TREE.  It is
*    about to be run with full privilege, and the data tree is writable by
*    every member of sdusers - shipping the script anywhere under it would let
*    one SD user rewrite what another user's elevated helper executes.
*    kernel(K$WINPATH) is what makes that reachable: the scripts are POSIX "/"
*    to SD and "C:\Program Files\SD" to PowerShell, which cannot open the
*    former.
*
* END-DESCRIPTION
*
* START-CODE

$internal
$flags trusted

function elevate(action, arg)
$catalog !elevate

$include int$keys.h

   set.status 0
   rc = 1

*  The two shipped scripts live beside sd.exe's tree root.  MAX.PATHNAME.LEN
*  applies to what comes back, and an empty answer means the runtime could not
*  convert - refuse rather than guess at a pathname that is about to be run
*  with privilege.
   ps1 = kernel(K$WINPATH, '/sd-elevate.ps1')
   if ps1 = '' then
      set.status 1
      return rc
   end

*  29 Aug 26 Windows port - ONE HELPER PER USER, NOT PER SESSION.  Owner's
*  instruction; PRE_RELEASE_FIXES 56.  @userno used to be in this name, so
*  every sd.exe got a helper of its own and therefore a UAC prompt of its own.
*  With 56 elevating an administrator AT LOGIN that became one consent per
*  command, which is the thing CLAUDE.md's "remove the need for a prompt, not
*  the step" section exists to prevent.  Dropping @userno lets the second
*  session find the first one's helper through PING and ask for nothing.
*
*  @logname STILL KEEPS TWO USERS APART, and the pipe's own ACL is what
*  actually enforces it - the helper grants its own user's SID and nobody
*  else's, so a wider NAME does not widen who can reach it.
*
*  THE LIFETIME RULE IS NOT WEAKENED, ONLY GENERALISED.  The helper still dies
*  with its sessions; it just has more than one now.  Each START and RUN
*  registers this session's pid with it and STOP deregisters, so it exits when
*  the LAST session using it has gone.  See sd-elevate-helper.ps1.
   pipe = 'sd-elev-' : @logname

   q = char(39)

*  THE AMPERSAND FORM DOES NOT PROPAGATE AN EXIT STATUS BY ITSELF - measured
*  14 Aug 2026 and recorded in !ps_script's header, where a script ending
*  "exit 7" reported 1.  Exiting explicitly with $LASTEXITCODE is what carries
*  the helper's answer back, and RUN's whole value is that answer.
   cmd = '& ' : q : ps1 : q

   begin case
      case action = 'START'
         cmd := ' -Start -PipeName ' : q : pipe : q
         cmd := ' -OwnerPid ' : kernel(K$WINPID, 0)

      case action = 'RUN'
         if arg = '' then
            set.status 1
            return rc
         end
         cmd := ' -Run -PipeName ' : q : pipe : q
         cmd := ' -Script ' : q : arg : q
* 29 Aug 26 Windows port - RUN CARRIES THE PID TOO, and that is not tidiness.
*   A session that reaches SDSYS while a helper is ALREADY running never calls
*   -Start, because START short-circuits on finding one - so RUN is the only
*   place some sessions ever announce themselves.  Without this the helper
*   would exit when the session that happened to start it left, taking the
*   privilege of every other session with it.  PRE_RELEASE_FIXES 56.
         cmd := ' -OwnerPid ' : kernel(K$WINPID, 0)

      case action = 'STOP'
         cmd := ' -Stop -PipeName ' : q : pipe : q
* 29 Aug 26 Windows port - AND STOP DEREGISTERS ONE SESSION RATHER THAN
*   STOPPING THE HELPER.  With one helper per user, CPROC's elevate('STOP') on
*   the way out of SDSYS would otherwise tear down the helper that other
*   sessions are still using.  The helper exits when its LAST owner goes.
         cmd := ' -OwnerPid ' : kernel(K$WINPID, 0)

      case 1
         set.status 1
         return rc
   end case

   cmd := '; exit $LASTEXITCODE'

   os.execute cmd
   rc = os.error()

return rc
end

* END-CODE
